 the most recent release that was healthy in production. Follow-up: confirm the error rate is back to normal and find the root cause in v5.1.0 before redeploying.", "idempotency_key": "payments-api-v5.1.0-rollback-sev2"}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_012LZCAEJKdXaR9dfLxV65j3", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments back to its last healthy release and opened the SEV2. The error rate is back to normal.\n\n- **Service:** No service is called just \"payments\". The affected one is **payments-api**. This morning's release, v5.1.0, was running in all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west) and was unhealthy, with an error rate of about 18%.\n- **Rollback:** I moved payments-api back to **v5.0.0** in all production regions. Its release history shows it as the most recent version that was healthy in production. It no longer has a \"Rolled out\" status. Because v5.0.0 was healthy before, this didn't need change-manager approval.\n- **Check:** After the rollback the service reports healthy, with an error rate of about 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** I opened **INC-0001** (SEV2, payments-api), which paged on-call. It notes that someone needs to find the root cause in v5.1.0 before it's deployed again.", "type": "text"}]}]}